WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.467 --> 00:00:09.033
This is Corpus: one workspace to ask your documents and query your data, running entirely in your browser.

2
00:00:09.767 --> 00:00:17.500
There's no sign-up and no API key. Open the app, and try it with the sample files.

3
00:00:18.233 --> 00:00:31.967
Three design documents and a pipeline-runs dataset load in seconds. On the right, the pipeline panel shows every stage: parsing, chunking, embeddings on the GPU, and the index.

4
00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:41.433
Ask what the post-mortem is about, and Corpus explains the document in plain words, instead of just listing topics.

5
00:00:42.167 --> 00:00:49.033
Every claim is cited. Hover a citation to see the exact sentence behind it.

6
00:00:49.767 --> 00:00:54.967
Click it, and the source passage opens with that sentence highlighted.

7
00:00:55.700 --> 00:01:07.800
Questions about numbers go to DuckDB. Ask how many runs failed per pipeline: Corpus writes the SQL, runs it on your machine, and charts the result.

8
00:01:08.533 --> 00:01:18.167
The data tab profiles each table as it arrives: missing values, outliers, unique keys, and charts chosen from the column types.

9
00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:24.933
Ask in plain English, then read, edit and re-run the SQL yourself.

10
00:01:25.667 --> 00:01:34.433
From that profile, Corpus writes a data contract: a dbt schema file with tests, ready to drop into a pipeline.

11
00:01:35.167 --> 00:01:46.900
And retrieval is measured, not assumed. Golden questions score keyword, vector and hybrid search, and the hybrid finds every answer first time.

12
00:01:47.633 --> 00:01:58.467
For written answers, run Google's Gemma 4 free on your own GPU, or bring a free key. Your files never leave your device.

13
00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:05.900
That's Corpus, built by Aditya Malviya. Try it live, at the link below.
